# Building Gantry apps

Gantry is a Go desktop-app framework: the app is one Go module containing React files, compiled by the `gantry` CLI into a single exe with the frontend embedded. Every feature is a **pair** - a folder holding a `.go` file and a `.tsx` file with the same name, talking over one websocket. The native window is a webview (WebView2 on Windows, WebKitGTK on Linux); the Go side owns logic and state, the tsx side owns the look.

## Golden rules

- **Never edit generated files**: `gantry_registry.go`, `gantry_widgets.go`, `gantry_icons.go`, `gantry_resources.go`, `gantry_args.go`, and everything under `.gantry/`, `webdist/`, `dist/`. They are rewritten on every `gantry dev` / `gantry build` / `gantry gen` - edits silently revert.
- **After adding, renaming, or removing a page/component/layout folder, run `gantry gen`** (or just start `gantry dev`, which does it automatically). Registration is generated by scanning the folders, never written by hand.
- **The pair key is the folder path** (`pages/index`, `components/example`). The Go half declares it explicitly (`Key: "pages/index"`); the tsx half calls `usePaired()` with **no argument** - the Vite plugin injects the key from the file location. Outside `pages/`/`components/`/`layouts/`, pass the key explicitly: `usePaired("pages/index")`.
- **Consult the embedded docs before guessing at Gantry APIs** - see the next section.

## Looking things up

The full framework documentation ships inside the `gantry` CLI. When the **gantry-docs MCP server** is available, prefer it:

- `search_docs` with keywords **or a pasted error message** - returns ranked snippets with routes.
- `read_doc` with a route from the results (e.g. `/ui/dynamic-routes`) - returns the full page as markdown.
- `list_docs` - the complete page map (`- Title (/route) - Category`); call it once when you need the territory.

Without MCP, print the best-matching page to stdout: `gantry docs --print <topic>` (e.g. `gantry docs --print dynamic routes`). The docs cover seven categories: getting-started, shell (window/tray/widgets/notifications), ui (pairs/pages/state/tea/styling), mobile, testing, cli, and advanced (architecture/protocol/errors).

## Project layout

```
myapp/
  main.go              gantry.Run(gantry.Config{...}) - window, tray, Setup hook
  gantry.json          app config (name, title, port, build targets, ...)
  index.css            global styles (plain CSS or Tailwind)
  go.mod  package.json embed.go  tsconfig.json
  pages/
    index/index.go     the Go half of a page   (exports var Page = ui.Page{...})
    index/index.tsx    the React half
    index/index.css    optional, auto-imported and scope-classed
  components/<name>/   reusable pairs           (exports var Component = ui.Component{...})
  layouts/<name>/      shared chrome around pages
  tests/               go tests run by `gantry test` via the gantrytest driver
  icons/  resources/   app icon; files embedded via the resources API
  gantry_*.go .gantry/ webdist/ dist/    GENERATED - never edit
```

One page (`pages/index`) means no router; the moment a second page folder exists, routing switches on and `pages/settings` serves at `/settings`. Folders nest to any depth, and bracket folders make dynamic routes (`pages/user/[id]`).

## The pair model

`usePaired()` returns exactly four members:

- `send(event, payload?)` - fire a named handler on the Go half; one-way, no return.
- `call(name, payload?)` - await a named Go function and get its result (pair-scoped `ui.Calls`).
- `on(event, fn)` - subscribe to named pushes from Go; returns an unsubscribe function (return it straight from a `useEffect`).
- `state` - the latest payload Go pushed under the event name `"state"`, mirrored into React state.

`send`/`call`/`on` are memoized on the key, so they are safe `useEffect` dependencies. Go pushes with `app.Push(key, event, payload)` - fire-and-forget, fanned out to the connected client. Go handlers run **inline on the websocket read loop**: do quick work directly, spawn a goroutine for anything slow. A panicking handler is recovered and reported through the error pipeline, not fatal.

## Two page styles (mix freely, per pair)

**Tea** - UI logic and state live in Go; React just renders the tree. State survives frontend reloads and tests drive it without a browser:

```go
package stats

import "github.com/BlueBeard63/Gantry/ui"

var Page = ui.Page{
	Key:   "pages/stats",
	Model: func() ui.Model { return model{} },
}

type model struct{ count int }
type incMsg struct{}

func (m model) Init() ui.Cmd { return nil }
func (m model) Update(msg ui.Msg) (ui.Model, ui.Cmd) {
	switch msg.(type) {
	case incMsg:
		m.count++
	}
	return m, nil
}
func (m model) View() ui.Node {
	return ui.Column(ui.Button("count up", incMsg{})).WithProps("class", "counter")
}
```

```tsx
// pages/stats/stats.tsx
import { TeaView } from "gantry-web/tea";
export default function Stats() {
  return <TeaView />; // renders whatever View() returns; wrap in any React you like
}
```

**Plain** - the UI is normal React; Go is the backend reached through handlers:

```go
package stats

import (
	"encoding/json"

	"github.com/BlueBeard63/Gantry/ui"
)

var Page = ui.Page{
	Key: "pages/stats",
	On: ui.Handlers{
		"save": func(p json.RawMessage) {
			var body struct {
				Name string `json:"name"`
			}
			_ = json.Unmarshal(p, &body)
			// quick work here; goroutine anything slow
		},
	},
}
```

```tsx
// pages/stats/stats.tsx
import { usePaired } from "gantry-web";
export default function Stats() {
  const { send, state } = usePaired();
  return <button onClick={() => send("save", { name: "jack" })}>save</button>;
}
```

Choose tea when the logic/state belongs in Go (one language, reload-proof state, testable without a browser); choose plain when the feature is browser-flavored UI with Go as its backend. Both styles can coexist on one pair.

## Adding a page or component

1. Create the folder and same-named files: `pages/stats/stats.go` + `stats.tsx` (+ `stats.css` for styles).
2. In the `.go` file: `package stats` exporting `var Page = ui.Page{...}` (or `var Component = ui.Component{...}` under `components/`).
3. Run `gantry gen` (or let `gantry dev` do it). Routing, registration and CSS scoping are automatic.

Components are used from tsx by normal import, or from a tea `View()` via `ui.Custom("components/example", nil)`. App-wide functionality (auth, settings, shared state, HTTP routes) goes in `main.go`'s `Setup` hook: `app.Service(name, ui.Calls{...})`, `ui.NewState(app, key, initial)`, `mux.HandleFunc(...)` - see the services, state, and http-endpoints docs.

## CLI commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `gantry new <name>` | Scaffold a new app (interactive; flags for tray/style/tailwind). |
| `gantry dev` | Run with live reload in the native window; regenerates derived files. |
| `gantry build` | Single exe per target into `dist/<os>/<arch>/`; `--installer` adds install artifacts. |
| `gantry test [pattern]` | Build and drive the real app through the tests in `tests/`. |
| `gantry gen` | Regenerate `gantry_*.go` derived files by hand. |
| `gantry add <pkg...>` | `npm install` into the app. |
| `gantry install --tailwind` | Retrofit Tailwind into an existing app. |
| `gantry update` / `gantry upgrade` | Update the CLI itself / bring the app's packages and scaffold up to the CLI's version. |
| `gantry docs` | Browse docs (web viewer); `--print <topic>` for stdout, `--mcp` for the MCP server. |

## Testing

`gantry test` runs the Go tests in `tests/` against the **real built app** - real process, real websocket, no mocks:

```go
func TestCounter(t *testing.T) {
	t.Parallel()
	app := gantrytest.Launch(t) // builds + starts the app, cleans up after
	app.Ready("pages/index")    // mount the page like the frontend would

	tree := app.WaitTree("first render", func(n *gantrytest.Node) bool {
		return strings.Contains(n.Text(), "count is 0")
	})
	app.Click(tree.Find("button"))
	app.WaitTree("count advanced", func(n *gantrytest.Node) bool {
		return strings.Contains(n.Text(), "count is 1")
	})
	app.ExpectNoErrors() // nothing landed in the error pipeline
}
```

Give interactive elements a `data-testid` for stable selection. The driver also does element-level DOM driving in a real webview, screenshots/screencasts, widget snapshots, error assertions, and an HTML report - `search_docs` the testing category for details.

## gantry.json in brief

The nearest `gantry.json` marks the app root. Key fields: `name` (module/exe name), `title` (window caption), `port` (fixed loopback port), `version`, `icons`, `tailwind`, `tray`, `buttons` (minimize/maximize/close), `args` (typed CLI args for the app), `build` (targets, installer), `mobile`. `mode` and `style` only record the scaffold choices - they do not constrain what you add later.

## Common pitfalls

- An edit to a `gantry_*.go` file "did not stick" - it is generated; put the change in `main.go`, the pair files, or `gantry.json` instead.
- Go logs `ui: no handler for pages/x.y` - the tsx `send`/`call` name does not match the Go `Handlers`/`Calls` map, or the Key is wrong.
- `usePaired()` throws about a missing key - the file is outside `pages/`/`components/`/`layouts/`; pass the key explicitly.
- New page does not appear - the folder/file names must match (`pages/stats/stats.go`), the Go half must export `Page`/`Component`, and `gantry gen` (or a `gantry dev` restart) must run.
- On Linux, building an app needs the GTK/WebKitGTK dev packages (`read_doc /getting-started/installation`); installing the CLI itself does not.
